## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates the ability to design governance that aligns regulatory requirements with product velocity in a distributed, multi-region setup.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-domain decision rights and escalation paths
- SLOs/SLIs, error budgets, runbooks, postmortems
- Phased releases: canary, shadow traffic, regional residency checks
- Rollback strategies and incident playbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-gating check for progress
function canProceed(region, changes) {
  if (!regsVerified(region)) return false;
  if (latencyImpact(changes) > SLO.latency) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate regulatory verification across regions?
- How would you handle conflicting gate criteria between regions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to translate a performance problem into a concrete, actionable plan with beginner-level scope.

## Key Concepts

- Telemetry-driven optimization
- Safe experiments with feature flags
- Incident response basics
- Stakeholder communication

## Code Example

```javascript
// Tiny cache helper for hot paths
const cache = new Map();
async function getCached(key, fetch){
  if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
  const val = await fetch();
  cache.set(key, val);
  return val;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success if latency improvements reduce throughput?
- What would you change if a third-party dependency introduces higher error rates after caching is added?
